\section{Introduction}
Kursav (ISO 639-3: faj; also called Faita or Kulsab) is a nearly extinct language of the Madang family, spoken on the south bank of the upper Ramu River in Madang Province, Papua New Guinea. Wikipedia reports about 10 speakers as of 2015 and UNESCO classes it as severely endangered; older speakers use Gende and younger people speak only Tok Pisin. No school materials were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Kursav were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on Kursav: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kursav_language}
\item New Guinea World entry for Kursav: \url{https://newguineaworld.linguistik.uzh.ch/families/madang-upper-yuat-river/madang/west-madang/sogeram-and-tomui-rivers/sogeram-river/east-sogeram-river/kursav}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/2215?hl=es}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
