\section{Introduction}
Fang (ISO 639-3: fak) is a small Southern Bantoid language of southern Cameroon, with about 4,000 speakers reported for 2011; ``Fang'' is the name of the village where it is spoken. It is distinct from the much larger Bantu Fang (Pahouin) language of Equatorial Guinea and Gabon, and it is related to Bulu and Ewondo. The searches found only encyclopedic coverage and no school materials.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on Fang (Cameroon): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fang_language_(Cameroon)}
\item General Alphabet of Cameroon Languages, the national orthographic base that a Fang orthography would likely follow: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Alphabet_of_Cameroon_Languages}
\item For contrast only, the Wikipedia article on the separate Fang language of Equatorial Guinea and Gabon: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fang_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Nothing specific to ISO fak was found. Materials for the large Fang language of Gabon and Equatorial Guinea must not be used as if they were for this language. Cameroonian national-language practice (General Alphabet) is the starting point for any orthography work.
